introduce stochasticity into the states. To mitigate sensitive gradients through high-dimensional vision-based world models, we modify the gradient structure to descend towards valid plans while only requiring action-input gradients. Our approach can be viewed as a stochastic version of a non-condensed or collocation-based optimal controller. We provide theoretical justification and experiments on video-based world models, where our resulting planner outperforms existing planning algorithms like the cross-entropy method (CEM) and vanilla gradient-based optimization (GD) on long-horizon experiments, both in success rate and time to convergence. `\n\nAPA\n\n`Psenka, M., Rabbat, M., Krishnapriyan, A.S., Lecun, Y. & Bar, A.. (2026). Parallel Stochastic Gradient-Based Planning for World Models. Proceedings of the 43rd International Conference on Machine Learning, in Proceedings of Machine Learning Research 306:99906-99930 Available from https://proceedings.mlr.press/v306/psenka26a.html. `\n\n#### Related Material\n\n  * Download PDF\n  * OpenReview\n\nThis site last compiled Tue, 06 Oct 2026 16:06:32 +0000--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nParallel Stochastic Gradient-Based Planning for World Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.00475)\nciteturn31449academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Title: Parallel Stochastic Gradient-Based Planning for World Models ... Date: Sat Jan 31 02:57:47 2026 ... We propose a robust and highly parallelizable planner that leverages the differentiability of the learned world model for efficient optimization, solving long-horizon control tasks from visual input.\nTitle: Parallel Stochastic Gradient-Based Planning for World Models\nAuthors: Michael Psenka, Michael Rabbat, Aditi Krishnapriyan, Yann LeCun, Amir Bar\nDate: Sat Jan 31 02:57:47 2026\n\nWorld models simulate environment dynamics from raw sensory inputs like video. However, using them for planning can be challenging due to the vast and unstructured search space. We propose a robust and highly parallelizable planner that leverages the differentiability of the learned world model for efficient optimization, solving long-horizon control tasks from visual input. Our method treats states as optimization variables (\"virtual states\") with soft dynamics constraints, enabling parallel computation and easier optimization. To facilitate exploration and avoid local optima, we introduce stochasticity into the states. To mitigate sensitive gradients through high-dimensional vision-based world models, we modify the gradient structure to descend towards valid plans while only requiring action-input gradients. Our planner, which we call GRASP (Gradient RelAxed Stochastic Planner), can be viewed as a stochastic version of a non-condensed or collocation-based optimal controller. We provide theoretical justification and experiments on video-based world models, where our resulting planner outperforms existing planning algorithms like the cross-entropy method (CEM) and vanilla gradient-based optimization (GD) on long-horizon experiments, both in success rate and time to convergence.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nParallel Stochastic Gradient-Based Planning for World Models — Lacuna (https://lacuna.tiptreesystems.com/work/parallel-stochastic-gradient-based-planning-for-world-models/wrk_2b067986d52895b58ce8b1de567a4eb8)\nciteturn31449search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; # Parallel Stochastic Gradient-Based Planning for World Models ... This synchronizes the virtual plan with the actual world model dynamics, ensuring the final trajectory remains valid.\n\n# Parallel Stochastic Gradient-Based Planning for World Models\n\nMichael Psenka, Michael Rabbat, Aditi S. Krishnapriyan, Yann LeCun, Amir Bar\n\nICML 2026 · OpenReview\n\n## Introduction\n\nPlanning in complex, vision-based \"world models\" is a notoriously difficult challenge. A world model is a neural network trained to predict future states given a current image and an action. While these models are powerful, using them to plan long-horizon tasks often leads to slow computation and a tendency to get stuck in local minima.\n\nTraditionally, planning with world models relies on two main approaches:\n\n  1. Sampling (e.g., Cross-Entropy Method - CEM): This involves guessing multiple action sequences, simulating them, and refining the best ones. While robust, CEM scales poorly as the planning horizon increases.\n  2. Gradient Descent (GD): This uses backpropagation to adjust actions to minimize the distance to a goal. However, chaining predictions together via Backpropagation Through Time causes errors to compound. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: Parallel Stochastic Gradient-Based Planning for World Models. (https://dblp.uni-trier.de/rec/journals/corr/abs-2602-00475.html)\nciteturn31449search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # \"Parallel Stochastic Gradient-Based Planning for World Models.\"\n\n# \"Parallel Stochastic Gradient-Based Planning for World Models.\"\n\n  * help us\n\n    * How can I correct errors in dblp?\n    * contact dblp\n\nMichael Psenka et al. (2026)\n\n\n## Details and statistics\n\n  * show external API response\n\n    * JSON @ openalex.org\n\nsee also:\n\n    * API doc @ openalex.org\n\nDOI: 10.48550/ARXIV.2602.00475\n\n\nmetadata version: 2026-06-14\n\n  * Image: Informal and Other Publications\n\n    * view\n\n\ndblp key:\n\n      * journals/corr/abs-2602-00475\n\n\n      * https://dblp.org/rec/journals/corr/abs-2602-00475\n\nMichael Psenka, Michael Rabbat, Aditi S. Krishnapriyan, Yann LeCun, Amir Bar:\nParallel Stochastic Gradient-Based Planning for World Models. CoRR abs/2602.00475 (2026)\n\n\nlast updated on 2026-06-14 17:02 CEST by the dblp team\n\n all metadata released as open data under CC0 1.0 license---------------------------------------------------------------------